<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sarts</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sart</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sart was a name used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> for settled, town-dwelling and farming peoples, as distinct from nomads, especially in the oases of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fergana-valley">Ferghana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a>. Originally meaning 'merchant', it came to cover Turkic- and Persian-speaking settled Muslims. Russian officials used it as an ethnic label, but the Soviet authorities abandoned it in the 1920s, and most Sarts were counted as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a>.</p></div>
